## Supersymmetric backgrounds, path integrals, and S4/S3 formulas

Definitions needed before using these terms include: the dimension/signature and
orientation of spacetime; spin structure and spinor bundles; the gauge principal
bundle and connection; each matter representation and field statistics; the
off-shell multiplet and auxiliary fields; the R-symmetry and its background
connection; the supercharge(s) and their reality/normalization convention; and
the background Killing-spinor equations. A rigid supersymmetric background is
not merely a curved manifold: it is a metric plus specified background
supergravity/R-symmetry/auxiliary fields admitting a nonzero solution of the
chosen fermion-variation equations. State the spin structure, reality contour,
R-charges and background fields, and explicitly compute the square `Q^2` as the
combination of isometry, gauge, R-symmetry and flavor transformations used in
the localization complex. Do not assume every metric admits such a background.

A partition function must name its field space, action, integration cycle,
gauge quotient/gauge fixing, measure or regulator, boundary conditions, and
parameters. A formal symbol `∫DΦ e^{-S[Φ]}` is not a constructed measure.
The usual localization step adds `t QV` and differentiates in `t`. This is a
formal physics Ward-identity derivation unless the regulated measure is
Q-invariant, the integration cycle has no boundary contribution, the integral
converges sufficiently to differentiate, the gauge/ghost complex is defined,
and the regulator and limit preserve these properties. An anomaly, contour
deformation, noncompact zero-mode direction, or instanton boundary can invalidate
the step. Name each such assumption rather than hiding it in “supersymmetry
protects the answer.”

The first carriers also need explicit definitions for these objects and their
domains: a **rigid limit** (which background supergravity fields are frozen and
which Planck-scale limit is taken); a **supersymmetry complex** (graded fields,
odd operator `Q`, its domain and exact square `Q^2`); a **gauge-fixed fluctuation
operator** (bundles, Sobolev/domain, ghosts and stabilizers); a **one-loop
determinant** (the ratio of specified bosonic/fermionic spectral determinants,
regularization, phase and zero-mode convention); and a **functional-integral
regulator** (finite mode/lattice/cutoff space, measure, contour and removal
map). For physical formulas, state whether fields and couplings are in SI or
natural units and give every suppressed constant. Under the common `ħ=c=1`
choice, a 4D gauge coupling is dimensionless, `a`, masses and `ε_i` have
mass dimension one, the sphere radius `r` has dimension length, and the
instanton fugacity `q` is dimensionless. Restore units explicitly if a result
is compared with an SI observable.

The finite-dimensional theorem and the field-theory formula have different
domains. The rigorous cohomological localization formula applies to a
finite-dimensional compact manifold (or a separately proved compact-support /
proper-pushforward extension) with smooth torus action, equivariant class and
invertible normal Euler classes after localization. A finite-dimensional
supersymmetric integral also needs the explicit cycle, measure, Ward identity
and decay in `thm-finite-dimensional-q-exact-deformation-invariance`. The
continuum field-space version must separately construct the infinite-dimensional
measure, gauge fixing, determinants, zero modes, instanton sectors, convergence
or a regulator-removal limit. None of the local finite-dimensional theorems
proves that a continuum functional integral exists.

For the round S4 example, Pestun's field-theory derivation for specified 4D
`N=2` vector/hyper theories produces the structural expression

`Z_{S^4} = (1/|W|) ∫_{\mathfrak t} da\, e^{-8π^2 r^2(a,a)/g_{YM}^2}\, Z_{1-loop}(ia)\, |Z_{inst}(ia, ε_1=r^{-1}, ε_2=r^{-1}, q)|^2`,

up to the paper's measure, root and coupling conventions. Here `G` is the named
compact gauge group, `T` a chosen maximal torus, `W` its Weyl group, `r` the
sphere radius, `a` the constant Cartan scalar at the saddle, `Z_{1-loop}` the
gauge-fixed fluctuation determinant, and the north/south pole factors count
instantons and anti-instantons in opposite Ω-backgrounds. A carrier would need
to state the exact representation content, masses, coupling, contour and
normalization before asserting a formula. In these conventions `q=e^{2πiτ}` is
the dimensionless instanton parameter for the declared complexified coupling
`τ`; `ia` records the paper's contour/analytic-continuation convention, and
`(a,a)` uses its specified invariant quadratic form. The formula is a source-reported
formal field-theory result in this scaffold, not a proved continuum theorem.
Pestun explicitly says that the noncommutative S4 deformation is not explicitly
constructed in the paper and assumes it is well-defined and agrees with the
Nekrasov R4 deformation near each pole. That is a material condition, not a
minor footnote.

For a 3D `N=2` theory on the round S3, the Kapustin–Willett–Yaakov calculation
reduces its specified superconformal Chern–Simons–matter model to a Cartan
integral of the form

`Z_{S^3} = (1/|W|) ∫_{\mathfrak t} da\, e^{-S_{cl}(a)} Z^{vec}_{1-loop}(a) Z^{matter}_{1-loop}(a;R)`,

where the vector determinant includes the root/Vandermonde factor and the matter
determinant depends on the declared representations and R-charges `R`. Exact
signs, `2π` factors, level quantization, FI terms and contour depend on the
authors' convention and theory; read and reproduce that convention before
making a formula a local item. KWY's cited result is for superconformal
Chern–Simons–matter theories (including their explicit U(N) and ABJM cases),
not every 3D supersymmetric QFT. Again, it is a field-theoretic localization
calculation, not proof of a general continuum measure. The schematic integral
sets the sphere radius to one; when restoring a radius, state the rescaling of
the Cartan scalar and all dimensionful masses/FI parameters. State the integer
Chern–Simons level and the exact R-charge convention of each chiral multiplet.

The mathematical one-loop route should be split into separate obligations:
define the boson/fermion fluctuation complex and gauge-fixing ghosts; prove the
linearized `Q` complex and its symbol/ellipticity or transverse ellipticity;
identify actual zero modes and remove/gauge them with correct stabilizers;
compute its equivariant character/index under explicit hypotheses; derive the
nonzero-mode determinant ratio from a finite regulator or a stated spectral
regularization; check regularization-scale/anomaly terms; and normalize the
orientation/determinant line. Cite the exact `Q^2` action and ensure all
operators commute with the chosen symmetry. Do not infer the determinant from
the index alone.

## Instanton counting

Define an instanton as a connection `A` on a specified principal bundle over
oriented Euclidean four-space (or a compactified four-manifold), with the
chosen sign of `F_A=±*F_A`, finite action and declared framing/gauge behavior.
Define its integer charge using the exact trace representation and coefficient
normalization. For `U(N)` on `R^4`, Nekrasov's ADHM construction uses finite
dimensional data with `V=C^k`, `W=C^N`, maps `B_1,B_2∈End(V)`, `I∈Hom(W,V)`,
`J∈Hom(V,W)`, complex and real moment-map equations and a stability condition;
the quotient is by `U(k)`. Specify the rotation torus `T^2` with weights
`ε_1,ε_2`, the framing torus and the fixed-point character. In the resolved
ADHM model, fixed points are indexed by `N`-tuples of Young diagrams; the
Euler/virtual weights produce the coefficient of the formal instanton series.

The safe mathematical formula is a **defined equivariant pushforward**
`Z_{inst}(a,ε_1,ε_2;q)=Σ_{k≥0}q^k∫^{eq}_{\mathcal M_{k,N}}1`, only after defining
`\mathcal M_{k,N}`, the equivariant coefficient ring, a legitimate pushforward
for this noncompact space, and the regularization/compactification. It may be
only a formal power series in `q`. Nekrasov emphasizes both the UV small-size
and IR noncompactness of instanton moduli and the use of an Ω-background and
noncommutative/FI deformation to control them. These are separate from a proof
that a continuum 4D gauge-theory path integral equals the finite-dimensional
ADHM count.

Complete local proof obligations for the mathematical counting branch are:
construct the ADHM quotient and show its smoothness under the stated stability
parameter; prove its relation to framed ASD/SD connections; compute the
`T^2×T_G` fixed set and tangent weights; prove the appropriate equivariant
localization/pushforward; check compactness/properness or the exact localized
coefficient convention; state the formal/analytic status of the `q`-sum; and
prove independence from any auxiliary resolution only when an actual
comparison theorem is supplied. The existing ordinary ASD index item gives
neither ADHM classification nor the equivariant pushforward.

## BPS states, central charges, solitons and monopoles

The sibling supersymmetry note owns the super-Poincaré algebra and the
model-specific operator positivity derivation of `M≥|Z|`. This note consumes
that precise interface only after it states the spacetime dimension/signature,
supercharge anticommutators, normalization and allowed central/topological
charges. A BPS state must be defined as a state in a specified Hilbert-space
representation saturating the bound, equivalently annihilated by the relevant
positive supercharge combination. “BPS multiplet,” “shortening,” “protected
sector,” “central charge,” “charge lattice” and “index” each need exact model
definitions. A degeneracy is not generally an index, and an index can exhibit
cancellation or continuum contributions.

Define the Witten index only when the trace exists, for example
`Tr_H((-1)^F e^{-βH})` on a specified `Z_2`-graded Hilbert space with
trace-class heat kernel; then prove its `β`-independence using the exact
supercharge relation and trace assumptions. A superconformal index additionally
specifies `H(S^3)`, the chosen supercharge, the positive anticommutator whose
kernel is counted, all commuting fugacity charges, and convergence/regularity.
It is not the dimension of the full Hilbert space. In 4D `N=2` a second
helicity supertrace or refined protected spin character needs its exact angular
momentum/refinement convention and a proof that the chosen sector is protected.

For a classical monopole example, fix a `3+1`-dimensional Minkowski model with
compact gauge group, adjoint Higgs field `φ`, gauge connection `A`, coupling,
zero Higgs potential (Prasad–Sommerfield limit), finite-energy static fields
and a chosen vacuum `φ_∞` that breaks `G` to a declared unbroken subgroup.
Define `B_i=\frac12 ε_{ijk}F_{jk}`, `D_iφ`, the asymptotic map/transition data
and magnetic charge `m=∫_{S^2_∞}⟨φ,B_i⟩dS_i`. For the action convention
`E=(1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}(|B|^2+|Dφ|^2)d^3x`, put `T=g^{-2}m` and require
smooth fields and falloff such
that `D_iB_i=0`, `D_iφ→0` at infinity, the asymptotic magnetic field lies in
the unbroken algebra, the surface flux exists, and integration by parts has no
residual bulk/boundary term. With an invariant inner product the displayed
surface charge is gauge invariant under the allowed asymptotic gauge group.
Then both square completions hold:

`E = (1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}|B-Dφ|^2d^3x + T`,

`E = (1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}|B+Dφ|^2d^3x - T`.

For an explicit proof, expand each square on a finite ball `B_R`; invariance
of the Lie-algebra inner product gives
`∂_i⟨φ,B_i⟩=⟨D_iφ,B_i⟩+⟨φ,D_iB_i⟩=⟨D_iφ,B_i⟩`, using the Bianchi identity.
Apply the divergence theorem on `B_R`, then take `R→∞` using the declared
falloff and surface-flux limit. The identities give `E≥|T|`; for `T>0`,
equality requires `B=Dφ`, and for `T<0`, equality requires `B=-Dφ`. A
zero-charge equality case must be handled separately. Tong's lecture uses
`1/e^2` in front of the quadratic energy and `2/e^2 m` for its boundary term;
it matches this normalization with `g^2=e^2/2` and the same invariant inner
product. A carrier must also derive
the gauge-invariant flux quantization from the declared global gauge group,
unbroken subgroup, bundle transition function and matter charge lattice; it
must not import a universal normalization of magnetic charge. This is a
conditional classical energy bound and first-order Bogomolny equation, not an
experimental observation and not a quantum particle state. The repository's
Abelian EM flux example is only a mathematical patching/flux calculation, not
this non-Abelian field model.

Likewise define an instanton/soliton moduli space only after specifying the
PDE, gauge quotient, boundary conditions, regularity, charge and compactness or
compactification. If a physical mass saturates an algebraic central-charge
bound, that is a separate consequence of the named supersymmetry algebra and
the explicit classical or quantum model; a formal classical energy-square
calculation alone does not establish a quantum particle state.

## Wall crossing, quivers and spectral networks

The existing finite-quiver wall-crossing theorem has an exact but narrow scope:
finite-dimensional representations of a finite **acyclic** quiver over
`F_q`, upper-half-plane vertex charges, subobject-first Hall product, HN
filtrations and a dimension completion. Its Euler/skew integrated phase
products are equal for two charges; it includes wild quivers and strictly
semistable objects, but no CY3, orientation data, analytic convergence,
physical Hilbert space or BPS identification. The generated spectrum
automorphisms are formal degreewise. The quiver-with-potential CoHA branch is
different: it uses complex representation quotient stacks and the specified
rational equivariant Borel–Moore/vanishing-cycle coefficients. Keep those
coefficient theories separate.

The current carrier metadata adds an acceptance boundary to this scope: the
root `thm-finite-quiver-hall-wall-crossing`,
`thm-finite-quiver-euler-and-skew-integration-is-multiplicative`, and
`lem-quiver-hardernarasimhan-filtrations-exist-are-unique-and-descend` items
are marked published but show only `verification.precheck: pass`. The local
`prop-qftx-hall-spectrum-invariance` item itself has a delegated `verified`
stamp dated 2026-10-22, but it depends on that upstream chain. Preserve its own
stamp without treating it as an accepted proof supplier until the weaker
upstream evidence is reconciled. The finite-field scope and the missing
physical BPS-index dictionary remain separate limitations.

Before a physical wall-crossing formula, define an abelian/dg category or
specified quiver gauge model; its charge lattice `Γ`, antisymmetric Dirac
pairing, central charge `Z:Γ→C`, stability phase, stable/semistable objects,
Harder–Narasimhan property, support/completion conditions and chamber. Define
the protected index `Ω(γ;u)` from a specific Hilbert-space supertrace, its sign
and spin refinement. A wall is the locus where named charges align in phase.
Then state the product order, quantum-torus multiplication, factor
normalization and which index is conserved. A mathematical KS/DT theorem in a
3CY category and a physical 4D `N=2` BPS wall-crossing statement are not the
same theorem. The physical bridge listed in
`def-qftx-cy3-bps-integration-contract` additionally needs a proved comparison
of the physical protected sector with the chosen algebraic invariant. Until
that comparison exists for a named model, do not state the physical count as
an established corollary of finite-field Hall algebra.

For a spectral network, define a punctured Riemann surface `C`, a spectral
cover `Σ→C` (including ramification), the meromorphic differentials/Seiberg–
Witten data, phase `ϑ`, the trajectory ODE for each sheet pair, branch points,
junction rules, finite webs, charge lift to a cycle on `Σ`, central charge,
surface defect and framed/unframed index. State the regularity and asymptotic
conditions that guarantee an actual finite network. The GMN construction is
specialized to class-S theories and its own 2D–4D framed BPS setup. Their 2013
paper describes a geometric argument “if not quite a proof” for the KS wall
formula and explicitly lists unresolved at that date: extension to D/E, the
precise charge-lattice quotient, a K>2 spectrum-generator construction,
refined spin/motivic indices, a precise space of spectral networks, and the
cluster-coordinate conjecture. Those are dated 2013 statements, not a 2026
status determination; review each against later sources before classifying it
as open. A 2026 spectral-network preprint listed below is a candidate source
for an updated finite-web result, but only its abstract was read here.

The `T_3` finite-web calculations in that preprint could supply a useful
dependency-ordered example once independently reviewed. They cannot supply a
general spectral-network theorem, a global BPS spectrum algorithm, or a
physical index identification. Mathematical claims about finite webs should
be proved in a mathematics-domain item; physical interpretation stays a
separately stated conditional/model-specific relation.

## Source and status record

Full-text reading here means the linked PDF was retrieved and selected sections
were inspected; it does **not** mean the entire source or every proof was
audited. The limits below are the actual reading scope for this note.

| Source | What was read and what it supports | Status and limits |
|---|---|---|
| V. Pestun, “Localization of Gauge Theory on a Four-Sphere and Supersymmetric Wilson Loops,” arXiv:0712.2824v3 (PDF title page 20 Sep 2012), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0712.2824` | Retrieved PDF; inspected abstract/contents and §1 passages containing (1.3)–(1.5), the S4 assumptions around the instanton correction, and the explicit regulator caveat. Section headings and equation locations for §§2–5 were searched to orient later reading, but their determinant/localization proofs were not read in this pass. | Not full paper or appendices. In particular, the author explicitly does not construct the noncommutative S4 deformation and assumes it is well-defined and agrees with Nekrasov's R4 deformation near the poles. Treat the path-integral identity as formal/conditional, not a rigorous continuum theorem. |
| A. Kapustin, B. Willett and I. Yaakov, “Exact Results for Wilson Loops in Superconformal Chern–Simons Theories with Matter,” arXiv:0909.4559v4 (PDF dated 17 Dec 2010), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4559` | Retrieved PDF; inspected abstract/contents, setup and S3 localization passages in §§2–3, including saddle locus, gauge fixing, zero modes and the Cartan matrix integral. | Not the complete determinant appendices or all examples. The claimed scope is superconformal Chern–Simons–matter, with explicit U(N)/ABJM examples; do not extend automatically to every 3D `N=2` theory. |
| G. Festuccia and N. Seiberg, “Rigid Supersymmetric Theories in Curved Superspace,” arXiv:1105.0689v2 (PDF dated 17 May 2011), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.0689` | Retrieved PDF; read the abstract, §1 and §2 general procedure including the auxiliary supergravity backgrounds and gravitino/Killing-spinor equations; inspected the stated S3×R/S3 qualifications. | Not a complete background classification. Their setup assumes the appropriate off-shell supergravity coupling and notes complex auxiliary/background values and reflection-positivity limitations. Each new background needs its own checked Killing-spinor solution. |
| N. Nekrasov, “Seiberg–Witten Prepotential from Instanton Counting,” hep-th/0206161 (18 Jun 2002), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0206161` | Retrieved PDF; read the introduction, §2.2 passages defining equivariant moduli-space integration, §§3.1–3.4 on ADHM, noncompactness/resolution and contour localization, and §4.2's equivariant K-theory/index view. Search hits in §3.5 locate the partition-indexed fixed-point discussion, but the tangent-weight derivation was not read in this pass. | Not a full audit of the gauge-theory path integral or every contour calculation. The paper itself identifies small-instanton and IR noncompactness, uses Ω/noncommutative regularization, and labels the KP/Toda relation conjectural. Instanton count is a formal/equivariant construction under its stated regulator, not a proof of a general continuum path integral. |
| D. Tong, “TASI Lectures on Solitons: Instantons, Monopoles, Vortices and Kinks,” arXiv:hep-th/0509216v5 (19 Dec 2005), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0509216` | Retrieved PDF; inspected §2.1–2.3 on SU(N) monopoles, charge sectors, Dirac quantization, the Bogomolny square completion and Prasad–Sommerfield example. | Pedagogical source, not an independent audit of all analytic existence/regularity theorems. The energy calculation is conditional on the specified classical action and asymptotics; the paper's 2005 observational statement is not carried forward as a current empirical-status claim. |
| D. Gaiotto, G. W. Moore and A. Neitzke, “Spectral Networks,” arXiv:1204.4824v2 (6 Oct 2013), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.4824` | Retrieved PDF; read abstract and introduction §§1–3, the open-problems list, and selected §6.5 wall-crossing discussion. | Not the complete derivations or all examples. The authors themselves call the geometric wall-crossing explanation “if not quite a proof”; all cited open directions are qualified as a 2013 source status. |
| M. Kontsevich and Y. Soibelman, “Stability Structures, Motivic Donaldson–Thomas Invariants and Cluster Transformations,” arXiv:0811.2435, `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0811.2435` | Retrieved PDF; inspected the table of contents and introductory §§1.1–1.6. The §2.1–2.3 stability-data/wall-crossing sections were identified by headings/search but their arguments were not read in this pass. | Not the proof sections for the motivic/3CY wall-crossing theorem. The paper's physical BPS comparison is motivation/interpretation; its motivic coefficient, orientation and integrality hypotheses must be read and checked before any exact reuse. The current repository's finite-field Hall proof is a separate local supplier. |
| M. Atiyah and R. Bott, “The Moment Map and Equivariant Cohomology,” *Topology* 23 (1984), 1–28, DOI `10.1016/0040-9383(84)90021-3`; D. Berline and M. Vergne, “Classes caractéristiques équivariantes. Formule de localisation en cohomologie équivariante,” *C. R. Acad. Sci. Paris* 295 (1982), 539–541. | Identified as primary mathematical sources for the compact finite-dimensional theorem; full texts were not retrieved/read in this research pass. | These are proposed source targets for authoring the local Borel/Cartan and fixed-point formulas. Do not claim a source section was read; verify exact theorem locators and hypotheses before item review. |

### Dated frontier scan (2026-10-23)

An arXiv API search was run on 2026-10-23. Only metadata and abstracts were
read; no full text, peer review, proof or independent priority check was done.
It gives useful *candidate* frontier updates, not accepted results:

- Omar Kidwai and Guillaume Tahar, “Spectral networks, finite webs, and the
  `T_3` cubic differential,” arXiv:2610.23752v1, submitted 2026-10-16,
  `https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23752v1`. The abstract claims a precise finite-web
  definition and results for one cubic differential on `P^1` with three
  double poles. Full-text review is needed before using any theorem or its
  physical dictionary.
- “Refined Vafa–Witten invariants for toric surfaces from supersymmetric
  localization in 5D gauge theory,” arXiv:2607.18410v1, submitted 2026-07-20,
  `https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18410v1`. The abstract says the relation of its
  even-first-Chern-class partition function to rational refined Vafa–Witten
  invariants remains unclear. Treat that as the authors' preprint status, not
  a community-wide resolution.
- “Localizing the M-Theory Path Integral,” arXiv:2609.31829v2, abstract
  updated 2026-10-07, `https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31829v2`. The authors call
  their formula a proposal for the local perturbative contribution. It does
  not justify a general rigorous continuum path integral in this library.
- GMN's own 2013 open-problem list remains a guide to questions whose later
  resolution must be checked: D/E-type networks, correct charge quotient,
  higher-rank spectrum generators, refined spin/categorified indices,
  convergence without defects, and cluster-coordinate status. The 2026 `T_3`
  abstract is relevant new evidence for one restricted higher-rank example,
  not evidence that those general questions have all been resolved.

For a genuine `op-` item, first formulate one exact question, inspect the
current full-text literature/status, separate already proved partial results,
and make the open item non-load-bearing. If literature establishes the result
but no local proof exists, record a proof-authoring obligation rather than an
open problem.

## Suggested dependency order and proof/formality ledger

1. Reuse the precise differential-geometric, gauge, characteristic-class and
   finite-regulator pages above. Add local Borel/Cartan cohomology, Borel
   localization, the fixed-component integration formula and equivariant
   index character. Resolve the existing Dirac supplier status before using
   its formula in any dependency closure.
2. Define the named rigid background and off-shell fields, solve its actual
   Killing-spinor equations, define `Q` and compute `Q^2`. Reuse the local
   BRST/anomaly interfaces only to the extent their Abelian setting matches;
   otherwise author a non-Abelian gauge-fixing and anomaly cancellation
   argument with its own hypotheses.
3. For finite regulators, define measure/cycle and prove the Q-Ward identity,
   deformation invariance, zero-mode/stabilizer handling and determinant
   normalization. A claim at this step can be a locally proved finite-model
   result if its regulator actually preserves the chosen symmetry.
4. Record S3/S4 formulas as source-reported conditional field-theory
   calculations until the functional measure and continuum passage are
   justified. For S4 include both instanton and anti-instanton pole sectors;
   for S3 state the exact matter, R-charge and level data.
5. For instantons, prove the fixed-dimensional ADHM/quiver data, torus weights
   and appropriate equivariant pushforward under a precise compactification
   or regulator. Do not call the resulting formal `q`-series a convergent
   partition function without a separate proof.
6. Build classical soliton/monopole claims from a fully specified action,
   boundary conditions and topological charge. Build quantum BPS claims only
   from the sibling's exact algebra/central-charge supplier and a defined
   protected Hilbert-space index.
7. Reuse the finite-field Hall/HN theorem and the CY3 bridge contract as
   mathematical/algebraic context. Any physical DT/BPS identity, all-regime
   chamber spectrum, refined index, or spectral-network reconstruction needs
   its own model-specific proof or a dated, source-reviewed open-problem
   record.

Classify claims as: **proved finite-dimensional mathematics** (for example,
the exact hypotheses of Borel localization once locally proved), **proved
model-conditional physics** (for example, the monopole energy bound from the
stated classical action), **formal/source-reported field-theory derivation**
(S3/S4 and instanton path-integral formulas), or **open/status-pending** (only
after the exact question and literature status are reviewed). No infinite-
dimensional continuum measure, universal localization identity, general
physical BPS count, or all-regime quiver/spectral-network claim is established
by this note.

