        "post-qftx-n2-pure-su2-ultraviolet-model"
      ],
      "statement": "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, spacetime $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and a selected pure four-dimensional\nrigid $\\mathcal N=2$ Yang--Mills model whose global gauge group is $SU(2)$.\nThe microscopic matter-hypermultiplet index set is empty, as explicitly adopted in the independent pure-model postulate. In $\\mathcal N=1$ language the gauge sector is one vector superfield and one adjoint chiral superfield with scalar $\\phi\\in\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$, connection normalization $\\phi_{\\mathrm{can}}=\\phi/g$, positive dimensionless gauge coupling $g$, zero chiral superpotential and no ultraviolet matter mass. The low-energy charged monopole fields introduced later are emergent infrared fields, not an ultraviolet hypermultiplet. This is the selected physical datum, not a specialization inferred from the different one-hypermultiplet postulate.\n\nA **classical Coulomb-branch configuration** is a constant adjoint scalar\nconfiguration satisfying the model's zero auxiliary-field conditions, taken\nmodulo the compact $SU(2)$ gauge action. This uses the compact-group orbit\nquotient in the preceding vacuum-moduli definition; it does not replace that\nquotient by a complexified-group stability construction. In the\nweak-coupling semiclassical frame, let $a$ be the eigenvalue coordinate fixed\nby $\\phi=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{diag}(a,-a)$, of mass dimension one. When a quantum\ntheory with a vacuum-moduli set has actually been constructed, the **quantum\nCoulomb-branch coordinate** $u$ denotes the chosen gauge-invariant chiral\nquadratic-operator expectation value in the source trace normalization for\nwhich the weak-coupling relation is $u=a^2/2$. Its mass dimension is two.\nThis definition does not assert that $u$ globally parameterizes the quantum\nvacua.\n\nOn an open patch $U$ of the $u$-plane where the period functions are\nholomorphic and the local gauge-coupling matrix has positive imaginary part,\nan **electric/magnetic frame** means a chosen ordered basis of the formal\ncharge lattice that fixes local cycle representatives. Their period\nfunctions are the complex electric coordinate $a:U\\to\\mathbb C$ and complex\ndual coordinate $a_D:U\\to\\mathbb C$, both of mass dimension one. The local\ntwo-derivative abelian gauge action is encoded by a holomorphic\n**prepotential** $\\mathcal F_U(a)$ of mass dimension two, defined on that\npatch by\n\n$$a_D=\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal F_U}{\\partial a},\\qquad \\tau=\\frac{\\partial a_D}{\\partial a}=\\frac{\\partial^2\\mathcal F_U}{\\partial a^2},\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}\\tau>0.$$\n\nThe dimensionless $\\tau$ is the local complexified gauge coupling; changing\nthe electric/magnetic frame changes the pair $(a_D,a)$ by the corresponding\nintegral linear transformation and may require a different local\nprepotential. Thus neither period is asserted to be a single-valued global\nfunction on a punctured branch.\n\nFor this source-normalized data, define the **formal electric/magnetic charge\nlabel lattice** to be $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{SW}}=\\mathbb Z^2$, with a label\n$(n_e,n_m)$ and period pairing\n$\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}(u;n_e,n_m)=n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)$. The pair labels\nthe central-charge formula; the lattice definition does not assert that every\nlabel is an allowed line operator, a dynamical particle, a stable state, or a\nstate in any constructed Hilbert space. Those are separate model data.\n\nThe word **exact** in this page refers only to the adopted source-proposed\nWilsonian two-derivative low-energy data on a regular patch of the selected\npure SU(2) model. It does not mean a proof of a nonperturbative continuum\npath integral, a global single-valued prepotential, or an exact statement\nabout arbitrary $\\mathcal N=2$ theories."
    },
    {
      "id": "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model",
      "kind": "definition",
      "dependency_level": 7,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-coulomb-branch-data",
        "post-qftx-n2-pure-su2-ultraviolet-model"
      ],
      "statement": "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and the local duality frame near one\nmonopole singularity of the selected pure $SU(2)$, four-dimensional rigid\n$\\mathcal N=2$ model. Let $A_D$ be the complex scalar coordinate in the\nlocal dual $U(1)_D$ vector multiplet, and let $M,\\widetilde M$ be complex\nscalar fields of dual electric charges $+1$ and $-1$ in two $\\mathcal N=1$\nchiral multiplets. These are emergent infrared monopole fields in a dual frame of the independent pure ultraviolet model, not microscopic matter hypermultiplets. The dual gauge group acts by\n\n$$A_D\\mapsto A_D,\\qquad M\\mapsto e^{i\\alpha}M,\\qquad \\widetilde M\\mapsto e^{-i\\alpha}\\widetilde M,\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z).$$\n\nLet $u(A_D)$ be the supplied holomorphic coordinate function on an open\npatch $|A_D|<r$ about $A_D=0$, with $u(0)=u_m$ and\n$c:=u'(0)\\ne0$. Let $\\mu\\ne0$ be the coefficient of the small adjoint\nmass perturbation, let $g_D>0$ be the dimensionless dual gauge coupling,\nand use connection normalization for $A_D$, canonical kinetic terms for $M,\\widetilde M$, and zero microscopic D-type Fayet--Iliopoulos parameter. Freeze the positive local vector-multiplet metric at $b=g_D^{-2}$; this is a specified constant-coupling two-derivative approximation within the patch, not the full field-dependent Wilsonian metric.\nDefine\n\n$$W_\\mu(A_D,M,\\widetilde M)=\\sqrt2 A_D M\\widetilde M+\\mu u(A_D),\\qquad D=g_D\\bigl(|M|^2-|\\widetilde M|^2\\bigr).$$\n\n$$V_\\mu=g_D^2|\\partial_{A_D}W_\\mu|^2+|\\partial_M W_\\mu|^2+|\\partial_{\\widetilde M}W_\\mu|^2+\\frac12D^2.$$\n\nLet $\\widetilde F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu\\widetilde A_\\nu-\n\\partial_\\nu\\widetilde A_\\mu$ and\n$\\widetilde D_\\mu A_D=\\partial_\\mu A_D$,\n$\\widetilde D_\\mu M=(\\partial_\\mu-i\\widetilde A_\\mu)M$, and\n$\\widetilde D_\\mu\\widetilde M=(\\partial_\\mu+i\\widetilde A_\\mu)\n\\widetilde M$. In metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, the\nconnection-normalized bosonic kinetic Lagrangian is\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{kin}}=-\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\left(g_D^{-2}\\partial_\\mu\\overline{A_D}\\,\\partial_\\nu A_D+\\overline{\\widetilde D_\\mu M}\\,\\widetilde D_\\nu M+\\overline{\\widetilde D_\\mu\\widetilde M}\\,\\widetilde D_\\nu\\widetilde M\\right)-\\frac{1}{4g_D^2}\\widetilde F_{\\mu\\nu}\\widetilde F^{\\mu\\nu}.$$\n\nThe mass dimensions are\n$[A_D]=[M]=[\\widetilde M]=[\\mu]=\\mathrm{mass}$,\n$[u]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$, $[W_\\mu]=\\mathrm{mass}^3$,\n$[F_{A_D}]=[F_M]=[F_{\\widetilde M}]=[D]=[\\widetilde F_{\\mu\\nu}]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$,\n$[\\widetilde A_\\mu]=\\mathrm{mass}$,\n$[c]=\\mathrm{mass}$, and $[g_D]=1$. The allowed regime is restricted to\nfield values within this patch and energies below its stated effective\ncutoff; “small $\\mu$” means that the induced scale\n$(|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2)^{1/2}$ lies well below that cutoff.\n\nFor a controlled leading linear-superpotential truncation, explicitly replace $u(A_D)$ by $u_m+cA_D$ in this constant-metric model. Then the perturbation is an $F$-type FI deformation of the local $\\mathcal N=2$ abelian theory, related to a D-type FI deformation by rotation of its auxiliary triplet. This extra truncation admits the critical BPS square completion. The generic holomorphic $u(A_D)$ model above is softly broken to $\\mathcal N=1$ and is not asserted to have exact BPS vortices; the full physical solution and a globally valid metric are separate inputs. Neither a small $\\mu$ nor a holomorphic superpotential theorem alone controls all non-holomorphic metric or higher-derivative corrections.\n\nThis is a one-singularity effective data package. It includes neither a\nsimultaneously local electric description of mutually nonlocal dyons nor a\nclaim that the local action extends over the full moduli space. A finite-energy\nvortex is not part of this Definition; its existence, when needed, is a\nseparate hypothesis."
    },
    {
      "id": "def-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-flux-tube-sector",
      "kind": "definition",
      "dependency_level": 8,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-coulomb-branch-data",
        "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model"
      ],
      "statement": "Use the dual $U(1)_D$ fields and potential of the preceding local\nmonopole-Higgs model. Let $x^1,x^2$ be coordinates on the transverse plane\n$\\mathbb R^2$ and let $x^3$ be the coordinate along a straight static tube.\nWrite $\\widetilde A_i$ for the spatial dual gauge potential,\n$\\widetilde F_{12}=\\partial_1\\widetilde A_2-\\partial_2\\widetilde A_1$,\n$D_iM=(\\partial_i-i\\widetilde A_i)M$, and\n$D_i\\widetilde M=(\\partial_i+i\\widetilde A_i)\\widetilde M$.\n\nA **finite-energy dual vortex profile of winding $n$** is a smooth static\nconfiguration $(A_D,M,\\widetilde M,\\widetilde A_1,\\widetilde A_2)$ on\n$\\mathbb R^2$ for which the transverse energy\n\n$$\\sigma=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{\\widetilde F_{12}^2}{2g_D^2}+g_D^{-2}|\\partial_i A_D|^2+|D_iM|^2+|D_i\\widetilde M|^2+V_\\mu-V_{\\mathrm{vac}}\\right)\\,d^2x$$\n\nis finite. The fields approach a supersymmetric vacuum at infinity satisfying\n$A_D=0$, $|M|=|\\widetilde M|=v>0$,\n$\\sqrt2 M\\widetilde M+\\mu u'(0)=0$, and\n$V_{\\mathrm{vac}}=0$. The phase map $M/|M|$ restricted to a sufficiently\nlarge circle has degree $n\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ as a map to the unit\ncircle; the phase map $\\widetilde M/|\\widetilde M|$ has degree $-n$.\nRequire also that $D_iM$ and $D_i\\widetilde M$ tend to zero on that circle,\nthat the coefficient of $d\\theta$ in the pulled-back one-form $\\widetilde A-d\\arg M$ tends uniformly in\n$\\theta$ to zero as the circle radius tends to infinity, and that the\nlimiting flux exists. The profile is a translation-invariant\ntube along $x^3$; its tension is the transverse integral $\\sigma$. This\nDefinition includes no assertion that such a configuration exists or is a\nstationary solution of the field equations.\n\nIn the adopted electric/magnetic frame, the **original electric flux** of\nthis sector means the dual magnetic flux\n$\\Phi_D=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{|x|\\le R}\\widetilde F_{12}\\,d^2x$.\nThe flux dictionary is part of the selected duality model; this Definition\ndoes not state that every electric line charge is represented by a vortex.\nIn natural units, $[\\widetilde A_i]=\\mathrm{mass}$,\n$[\\widetilde F_{12}]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$, and\n$[\\sigma]=\\mathrm{mass}^2$."
    },
    {
      "id": "post-qftx-n2-seiberg-witten-effective-solution",
      "kind": "postulate",
      "dependency_level": 8,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-coulomb-branch-data",
        "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model",
        "post-qftx-n2-pure-su2-ultraviolet-model"
      ],
      "statement": "Adopt the source-proposed low-energy solution data for pure four-dimensional\nrigid $\\mathcal N=2$ Yang--Mills with global gauge group $SU(2)$, one\nadjoint vector multiplet, rank one, no matter hypermultiplets, and the coupling, units, connection-normalized scalar, and prepotential convention of the independent pure ultraviolet vector-multiplet datum. Use natural units\n$c=\\hbar=1$, metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and a positive\ndynamical scale $\\Lambda$ of mass dimension one. The gauge-invariant\ncoordinate $u$ has mass dimension two; set $w=u/\\Lambda^2$.\n\nThe absence of microscopic hypermultiplets is an explicit model input in that independent ultraviolet postulate. The charged monopole hypermultiplet in a local infrared frame below is emergent; it does not change the ultraviolet matter content.\n\nOn the regular $w$-plane with singular values $w=1,-1,\\infty$ removed,\nadopt the family of genus-one curves, where $x,y,w$ are dimensionless:\n\n$$\\Sigma_w:\\quad y^2=(x-1)(x+1)(x-w).$$\n\nand the meromorphic period differential\n\n$$\\lambda_{\\mathrm{SW}}=\\frac{\\sqrt2\\Lambda}{2\\pi}\\frac{y\\,dx}{x^2-1}.$$\n\nWithin this proposed solution, take the quantum vacuum-moduli data to be the\nentire $u$-plane, with a $\\mathbb Z_2$ symmetry $u\\mapsto-u$ and singular\nvalues $u=\\Lambda^2,-\\Lambda^2,\\infty$. This is part of the adopted\nsolution ansatz, not a consequence of a constructed continuum theory.\n\nFor a chosen local cycle basis $\\gamma_m,\\gamma_e$ on $\\Sigma_w$ with\nintersection number $\\gamma_m\\cdot\\gamma_e=1$, define\n$a_D=\\oint_{\\gamma_m}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{SW}}$ and\n$a=\\oint_{\\gamma_e}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{SW}}$. These periods have mass\ndimension one, are multivalued under continuation around the punctures, and\nare used in the local prepotential convention\n$a_D=\\partial\\mathcal F/\\partial a$ and\n$\\tau=\\partial^2\\mathcal F/\\partial a^2$ with\n$\\operatorname{Im}\\tau>0$. In the source's counterclockwise-loop\nconvention, the period column $(a_D,a)^T$ has monodromy matrices\n\n$$M_\\infty=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad M_+=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\-2&1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad M_-=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\\\-2&3\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nAdopt the source's formal charge-label lattice $\\mathbb Z^2$ and central-charge pairing\n$\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}=n_ea+n_ma_D$ for labels\n$(n_e,n_m)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$. In the supersymmetry convention of this page,\nthe scalar central charge is normalized as\n$z=\\sqrt2\\,\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}$. The mass inequality and its\nsaturation statement are separate theorem deductions. No claim is made that\nevery lattice label is an allowed line operator or is represented by a stable\nparticle.\n\nAdopt the singularity interpretation that the $w=1$ point has a light\nmonopole with $a_D=0$, while the $w=-1$ point has a light dyon with\n$a-a_D=0$ in the chosen frame. These are source-proposed physical inputs\ntested by their monodromies; they are not deduced here from a constructed\ncontinuum theory.\nIn the local monopole patch, adopt the dual $U(1)_D$ field content and superpotential described in the preceding effective-model definition. The source two-derivative vector metric is positive and generally field dependent, determined by the local dual coupling; a regulated matching scale is part of any effective action with the light monopole retained. That definition explicitly freezes this metric at $b=g_D^{-2}>0$ for the subsequent classical calculations. Metric freezing, and the further replacement of $u(A_D)$ by its linear Taylor term where explicitly requested, are additional approximations, not exact identities of the full period solution. No bound on their discarded corrections is adopted here. A small N=1-breaking mass parameter $\\mu$ of\nmass dimension one adds the local superpotential term $\\mu u(A_D)$ to\n$\\sqrt2 A_D M\\widetilde M$. The local description applies only near this\nsingularity, below its effective cutoff, and does not simultaneously describe\nthe mutually nonlocal dyon at the other singularity.\n\nHere **exact low-energy solution** means the adopted two-derivative\nWilsonian data of this source-defined model on its regular patches. It does\nnot mean a proof of a nonperturbative UV path integral, a global\nsingle-valued prepotential, or an identity for arbitrary gauge groups,\nmatter contents, or N=2 theories."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-qftx-n2-bps-central-charge-mass",
      "kind": "physics-theorem",
      "dependency_level": 9,
      "deps": [
        "def-countable-choice",
        "def-qftx-n2-coulomb-branch-data",
        "def-qftx-unitary-super-poincare-representation",
        "post-qftx-n2-seiberg-witten-effective-solution",
        "thm-qftx-n-extended-central-charge-bounds"
      ],
      "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Use the four-dimensional rigid\n$\\mathcal N=2$ super-Poincare convention\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\epsilon_{12}=1$, and\n$c=\\hbar=1$. Let $U$ be a regular patch of the selected pure-SU(2)\nSeiberg--Witten period data. Fix $u\\in U$ and a formal charge label\n$(n_e,n_m)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$. Suppose the selected physical model has a\nnonzero massive unitary rest-fiber representation $\\mathcal K$ with mass\n$M>0$, scalar central-charge matrix $Z^{12}=zI$, $Z^{21}=-zI$, a common\ndense invariant product domain $\\mathcal D$, and actual Hilbert adjoints for\nall supercharges. Require its charge normalization to be\n\n$$z=\\sqrt2\\,\\mathcal Z_{\\mathrm{SW}}(u;n_e,n_m)=\\sqrt2\\bigl(n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)\\bigr).$$\n\nThen\n\n$$M\\ge\\sqrt2\\,|n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)|.$$\n\nIf equality holds, choose $\\varphi\\in\\mathbb R$ with\n$e^{-i\\varphi}z=|z|$ and define\n$A=Q^1_1-e^{i\\varphi}(Q^2_2)^*$ and\n$A^\\sharp=(Q^1_1)^*-e^{-i\\varphi}Q^2_2$ on $\\mathcal D$. Both\n$A\\psi$ and $A^\\sharp\\psi$ vanish for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D$.\nEquality defines algebraic BPS saturation only; the statement asserts no\nstate exists for any chosen charge label and no BPS state is stable."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-vortex-flux-and-tension",
      "kind": "physics-theorem",
      "dependency_level": 9,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-flux-tube-sector",
        "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model"
      ],
      "statement": "Use the local dual $U(1)_D$ model with natural units, positive coupling\n$g_D>0$, smooth gauge potential on $\\mathbb R^2$, and the vortex boundary\nconditions of the preceding definition. If a finite-energy dual vortex\nprofile of winding $n\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ exists, then its dual\nmagnetic flux, which is the original electric flux in the adopted duality\nframe, is\n\n$$\\Phi_D=2\\pi n,$$\n\nand its transverse tension satisfies $0<\\sigma<\\infty$. This is a\nconditional statement about an admitted profile; it asserts neither\nexistence nor stability of any vortex or a linearly confining minimum-energy\npotential between external charges."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-qftx-n2-monopole-condensation-after-n1-perturbation",
      "kind": "physics-theorem",
      "dependency_level": 11,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-coulomb-branch-data",
        "def-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-flux-tube-sector",
        "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model",
        "def-qftx-susy-vacuum-moduli-data",
        "post-qftx-n2-seiberg-witten-effective-solution",
        "post-qftx-n2-pure-su2-ultraviolet-model",
        "pthm-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-vortex-flux-and-tension",
        "pthm-qftx-n2-linear-monopole-bps-vortex-bound",
        "def-qftx-n2-nonlinear-magnetostatic-flux-cylinder",
        "pthm-qftx-n2-nonlinear-local-electric-flux-confinement",
        "def-axiom-of-choice"
      ],
      "statement": "Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$, metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and the local monopole patch of the\nselected pure-SU(2) N=2 source-proposed low-energy model. Let $u(A_D)$,\n$c=u'(0)\\ne0$, $M$, $\\widetilde M$, $g_D$, and $\\mu$ be the data in the\nlocal dual-Higgs definition, with $\\mu\\ne0$ and\n$(|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2)^{1/2}$ well below the effective cutoff. Restrict to a\nneighborhood of $A_D=0$ on which $u'(A_D)\\ne0$. The supersymmetric vacuum\nequations in this patch have exactly one $U(1)_D$ gauge orbit, represented\nby\n\n$$A_D=0,\\qquad M\\widetilde M=-\\frac{\\mu c}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad |M|=|\\widetilde M|=v:=\\left(\\frac{|\\mu c|}{\\sqrt2}\\right)^{1/2}>0.$$\n\nThus the local monopole fields have nonzero gauge-fixed amplitudes and\n$U(1)_D$ is fully Higgsed. With canonical charged-hypermultiplet kinetic terms in the\nconnection-normalized local model, the dual vector mass is\n\n$$m_D^2=2g_D^2\\bigl(|M|^2+|\\widetilde M|^2\\bigr)=4g_D^2v^2>0.$$\n\nFor an actual generic nonlinear confinement conclusion, use the precise magnetostatic flux-boundary class of the nonlinear cylinder definition, with the full $u$, a closed neutral disk on which $u'\\ne0$, positive metric bounds, constant or absent theta, uniformly vacuum-approaching profiles, and fixed canonical end traces. For this energy branch assume full AC, as required by its integration suppliers. The planar energy infimum $\\tau_n$ is positive and finite for every $n\\ne0$, and the full static-energy infimum obeys\n\n$$\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{I_n(L)}{L}=\\tau_n>0.$$\n\nThis proves asymptotically linear confinement of the specified original electric flux in that classical local effective model without assuming a noncritical stationary vortex or any attained minimum. It makes no generic $O(1)$ remainder claim.\n\nFor the stronger exact-remainder conclusion, make the following additional explicit\neffective-model assumption: for a fixed nonzero original electric test-charge\nsector and every sufficiently large separation $L$, the minimum-energy\nconfiguration in that sector is a straight, translation-invariant dual\nvortex of nonzero winding as defined here, and its endcap energy is bounded\nuniformly in $L$. If that flux-tube sector exists, its original electric\nflux is quantized and its tension is positive; the corresponding potential\nhas $V(L)=\\sigma L+O(1)$, where $O(1)$ denotes a term bounded uniformly as\n$L\\to\\infty$. This is linear confinement only in the stated local effective\nmodel and under the stated vortex/minimization assumption. The theorem does not prove that a vortex exists or minimizes the energy in the generic nonlinear model. In the separately specified constant-metric linear truncation $u=u_m+cA_D$, a supplied critical abelian-Higgs solution and fixed-flux finite-cylinder end traces with finite-energy collars suffice instead: the infimum of the full static energy is $4\\pi v^2|n|L+O(1)$ without assuming that a straight tube already minimizes the energy. Arbitrary nonzero winding and arbitrary supplied vortex centres are allowed. Existence of those planar solutions and of collars matching a chosen probe model remains an explicit prerequisite; exact BPS saturation is asserted only for this linear truncation."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-qftx-n2-linear-monopole-bps-vortex-bound",
      "kind": "physics-theorem",
      "dependency_level": 9,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model",
        "def-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-flux-tube-sector"
      ],
      "statement": "Use the constant-metric connection-normalized dual model, but explicitly take the linear truncation $u(A_D)=u_m+cA_D$, where $c\\ne0$, $\\mu\\ne0$, $g=g_D>0$. Put $v^2=|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2$, $\\xi=2v^2$ and $e^{i\\beta}=-\\mu c/(\\sqrt2v^2)$. Define charge-one fields\n\n$$q=\\frac{M+e^{i\\beta}\\overline{\\widetilde M}}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad p=\\frac{M-e^{i\\beta}\\overline{\\widetilde M}}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad s=|q|^2+|p|^2.$$\n\nLet $A=\\widetilde A_1dx^1+\\widetilde A_2dx^2$ be smooth and real on $\\mathbb R^2$, $D_i=\\partial_i-iA_i$, $B=\\partial_1A_2-\\partial_2A_1$ and $a=A_D$. Consider any smooth finite-energy profile in the winding-$n$ class of the flux-sector definition, $n\\ne0$, with $q$ winding $n$, $|q|\\to\\sqrt\\xi$, $p\\to0$ and $a\\to0$. In addition require the covariant boundary-current integral $\\oint_{|x|=R}(\\bar qDq+\\bar pDp)$ to tend to zero, and the square integrals below to exist; exponential covariant decay suffices. For $n>0$ its full transverse energy obeys\n\n$$E=2\\pi\\xi n+\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left\\{\\frac{[B-g^2(\\xi-s)]^2}{2g^2}+|(D_1+iD_2)q|^2+|(D_1+iD_2)p|^2+2g^2\\xi|p|^2+g^{-2}|\\partial_i a|^2+2|a|^2s\\right\\}d^2x.$$\n\nFor $n<0$ reverse the transverse orientation to obtain $E\\ge2\\pi\\xi|n|=4\\pi v^2|n|$. If a smooth critical abelian-Higgs solution $q,A$ with $|q|\\to\\sqrt\\xi$, winding $n>0$, the stated decay, and\n\n$$(D_1+iD_2)q=0,\\qquad B=g^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$$\n\nis supplied, then $a=p=0$, $M=q/\\sqrt2$, $\\widetilde M=e^{i\\beta}\\bar q/\\sqrt2$ is a stationary absolute transverse energy minimum among all the above full-model profiles with the same winding. Its tension is $T_n=2\\pi\\xi|n|$. This statement permits every nonzero integer winding and all supplied vortex centres; it does not prove existence of a critical solution.\n\nFor the classical static three-dimensional energy on $\\mathbb R^2\\times[0,L]$, impose the same flux $2\\pi n$ and decay on almost every transverse slice, no flux loss through transverse infinity, and nonnegative longitudinal covariant-gradient and Maxwell terms. Suppose each prescribed end trace can be joined to one chosen transverse minimizer by a supplied smooth collar of fixed length $\\ell$, finite energy $C_0$ or $C_1$, with the collar equal to the minimizer near its inner face and with the same flux on every slice. For $L\\ge2\\ell$, the infimum $I_L$ in this fixed-flux, fixed-end-trace class satisfies\n\n$$T_nL\\le I_L\\le T_n(L-2\\ell)+C_0+C_1\\le T_nL+C_0+C_1.$$\n\nThus $I_L=T_nL+O(1)$, with a bound independent of $L$. If both traces equal the minimizer and no additional end constraints are imposed, the translation-invariant extension is an exact minimum with energy $T_nL$. These are classical finite-cylinder boundary sectors. Equating them with a specified microscopic external-electric-probe sector requires a separate physical dictionary and boundary/collar construction; no point-probe self-energy subtraction or quantum Wilson-loop area law follows here. In linear response about the Higgs vacuum, original magnetic probes are dual electric sources for a vector of mass $m_D^2=2g^2\\xi$; its unit canonical static source potential is $e^{-m_Dr}/(4\\pi r)$, so that response is screened. The tube instead carries dual magnetic, hence original electric, flux in the adopted frame.\n\nFor a generic nonlinear $u$, the same embedded fields with $a=0$ have the pointwise potential derivative\n\n$$\\left.\\partial_{\\bar a}V_\\mu\\right|_{a=0}=g^2\\bar\\mu\\,\\overline{u''(0)}\\bigl(\\sqrt2M\\widetilde M+\\mu c\\bigr).$$\n\nWhenever this is nonzero at any point, the embedded profile is not stationary for the full nonlinear model. Small soft breaking alone does not make the exact linear-truncation BPS claim an exact claim about that full model."
    },
    {
      "id": "def-qftx-n2-nonlinear-magnetostatic-flux-cylinder",
      "kind": "definition",
      "dependency_level": 9,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-monopole-local-dual-higgs-model",
        "def-qftx-n2-dual-higgs-flux-tube-sector"
      ],
      "statement": "Use the local monopole fields, charges and superpotential with $a=A_D$, $c=u'(0)\\ne0$, $\\mu\\ne0$, $v^2=|\\mu c|/\\sqrt2$ and $\\xi=2v^2$. Keep the holomorphic function $u$ nonlinear. Choose a closed disk $K_\\delta=\\{|a|\\le\\delta\\}$ contained in its open valid patch with $u'(a)\\ne0$ throughout. Define\n\n$$m_*=\\min_{a\\in K_\\delta}|\\mu u'(a)|>0,\\qquad K=\\sup_{a\\in K_\\delta}|\\mu u''(a)|<\\infty.$$\n\nContinuity, compactness and the nonzero $c$ allow such a disk. No linear Taylor replacement of $u$ is made. In the already adopted constant-metric model put $b(a)=b_0=g_D^{-2}$. More generally, the same classical energy problem may be specified with a supplied smooth real function $b$ on a neighborhood of $K_\\delta$ and constants $0<b_-\\le b(a)\\le b_+<\\infty$. This is additional explicit local Wilsonian metric data, not the vector-only logarithmically singular period metric inferred at a massless-monopole point. Let $b_0=b(0)$ and $g_0^2=b_0^{-1}$. Define\n\n$$H=\\sqrt2M\\widetilde M+\\mu u'(a),\\qquad d=|M|^2-|\\widetilde M|^2,\\qquad s=|M|^2+|\\widetilde M|^2,$$\n\n$$V_b=b(a)^{-1}|H|^2+\\frac{d^2}{2b(a)}+2|a|^2s.$$\n\nCharged kinetic terms are canonical. The gauge kinetic coefficient is $b(a)$ and the neutral kinetic coefficient is $b(a)$. Work in the classical magnetostatic model with omitted or **constant** theta angle, no dynamical dual magnetic sources in the interior, static fields and $A_0=0$. No field-dependent-theta Gauss-law conclusion is included.\n\nFix $n\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $e^{i\\beta}=-\\mu c/(\\sqrt2v^2)$. A planar admissible profile is a smooth real one-form $A=A_1dx^1+A_2dx^2$ and smooth complex fields $a,M,\\widetilde M$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, with $a(x)\\in K_\\delta$, finite energy\n\n$$E_b=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left\\{\\frac{b(a)}2B^2+b(a)|\\partial_i a|^2+|D_iM|^2+|\\widetilde D_i\\widetilde M|^2+V_b\\right\\}d^2x,$$\n\nwhere $i=1,2$, $B=\\partial_1A_2-\\partial_2A_1$, $D_i=\\partial_i-iA_i$ and $\\widetilde D_i=\\partial_i+iA_i$. Require **uniform** approach to the vacuum orbit at transverse infinity:\n\n$$a\\to0,\\qquad |M|,|\\widetilde M|\\to v,\\qquad M\\widetilde M\\to e^{i\\beta}v^2.$$\n\nThis is an independent boundary hypothesis, not inferred from finite energy. Outside a compact set $M$ is nonzero and its phase has winding $n$ on each sufficiently large circle; the phase of $\\widetilde M$ has winding $-n$. In the angular coordinate $\\theta$, require the coefficient of $d\\theta$ in $A-d\\arg M$ to tend uniformly to zero, and the limiting flux to exist. Require also $D_iM,\\widetilde D_i\\widetilde M\\to0$ uniformly, as in the admitted flux-sector data. These conditions define the gauge-invariant class $\\mathcal P_n$. Gauge transformations are arbitrary smooth maps $U:\\mathbb R^2\\to U(1)$ acting by $M\\mapsto UM$, $\\widetilde M\\mapsto U^{-1}\\widetilde M$ and $A\\mapsto A-iU^{-1}dU$; there is no added asymptotic-constant gauge restriction. A **canonical exterior representative** has $M=m e^{in\\theta}$ with $m>0$ real outside a compact set.\n\nWrite\n\n$$\\tau_n=\\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal P_n}E_b(P).$$\n\nThis notation alone asserts neither nonemptiness nor attainment. The adopted electric/magnetic dictionary identifies the dual magnetic flux with the original electric flux only for the specified test-charge/flux boundary sector.\n\nFor fixed canonical exterior representatives $P_0,P_1\\in\\mathcal P_n$ and $L>0$, let $\\mathcal C_n(L;P_0,P_1)$ be the smooth static fields on $\\mathbb R^2\\times[0,L]$ with these tangential end traces, $a\\in K_\\delta$, and a planar admissible winding-$n$ profile on almost every slice. The flux $2\\pi n$ is thus fixed throughout the cylinder, with no flux leakage to transverse infinity or interior magnetic source. Allow $A_3$ but choose $A_3=0$ in trial collars. Define\n\n$$E_{b,3}=\\int_0^L\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left\\{\\frac{b(a)}2\\sum_{i<j}F_{ij}^2+b(a)\\sum_{i=1}^3|\\partial_i a|^2+\\sum_{i=1}^3\\bigl(|D_iM|^2+|\\widetilde D_i\\widetilde M|^2\\bigr)+V_b\\right\\}d^2x\\,dx^3,$$\n\nand $I_n(L;P_0,P_1)=\\inf_{\\mathcal C_n(L;P_0,P_1)}E_{b,3}$. Competitors have finite displayed energy and the stated flux/boundary data; no stationary solution or minimizer is assumed. Any further hard field cutoff is part of a different admissible class and must be checked to admit the profiles and interpolation paths used here. This functional is the specified local classical model; no error control to a microscopic action or quantum Wilson-loop interpretation is asserted."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-qftx-n2-nonlinear-local-electric-flux-confinement",
      "kind": "physics-theorem",
      "dependency_level": 10,
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-n2-nonlinear-magnetostatic-flux-cylinder",
        "def-axiom-of-choice",
        "thm-general-stokes-theorem",
        "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
        "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
      ],
      "statement": "Assume full Axiom of Choice. Use the nonlinear local magnetostatic data, class $\\mathcal P_n$ and fixed-flux cylinders defined above. In particular the neutral scalar lies in the specified small closed disk, the positive smooth **light-hyper-retaining** metric has the explicit bounds $b_-,b_+$, the vacuum approach is uniform, and theta is omitted or constant. Let\n\n$$t_*=\\min\\{\\xi/2,m_*/\\sqrt2\\}>0,\\qquad C_V=\\max\\left\\{\\frac{4b_+\\xi^2}{m_*^2},\\;6b_++\\frac{2K^2}{t_*}\\right\\},\\qquad c_V=C_V^{-1},\\qquad \\gamma=\\min\\left\\{1,\\frac{b_-}{b_0},2b_0c_V\\right\\}>0.$$\n\nThen the full nonlinear potential and every planar profile obey\n\n$$V_b\\ge c_V(s-\\xi)^2,\\qquad E_b\\ge\\gamma\\,2\\pi\\xi|n|.$$\n\nThe class $\\mathcal P_n$ is nonempty for **every** nonzero integer winding: a smooth compact-core radial test suffices, with no field equation, exact BPS profile or Taubes existence theorem assumed. Consequently\n\n$$0<\\gamma\\,2\\pi\\xi|n|\\le\\tau_n<\\infty.$$\n\nEvery admissible profile has a globally smooth gauge transform to a canonical exterior representative. Any two such canonical representatives admit a smooth fixed-width finite-energy interpolation collar in the same winding class; the collar keeps the neutral scalar in its convex disk. In particular, for fixed end traces $P_0,P_1$ and any $\\varepsilon>0$, there are finite constants $C_\\varepsilon$, $\\ell>0$, independent of $L$, such that for $L\\ge2\\ell$\n\n$$\\tau_nL\\le I_n(L;P_0,P_1)\\le(\\tau_n+\\varepsilon)(L-2\\ell)+C_\\varepsilon.$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{I_n(L;P_0,P_1)}{L}=\\tau_n>0.$$\n\nThis is asymptotically linear classical confinement of the specified original electric flux in the stated local magnetostatic boundary model. It does not assert that the planar infimum or finite-cylinder infimum is attained, that a nonlinear stationary profile is BPS, or that $I_n(L)=\\tau_nL+O(1)$. The end-collar constants may depend on the chosen $\\varepsilon$-profile. Exact critical $O(1)$ bounds require the stronger critical minimizing-profile input, kept separately. No conclusion follows for arbitrary microscopic line charges, flux-changing processes, a field-dependent theta term, or a quantum Wilson-loop area law."
    }
  ],
