+      locator: "§1, equations (1.3)–(1.4); §4, equations (4.47) and (4.50); §5, equations (5.1), (5.11)–(5.12), and the noncommutative-regulator qualification."
+    - title: "Vasilis Peelaers, 4d N=2 Localization"
+      url: https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/3026/attachments/2163/2457/Peelaers-LectureNotes.pdf
+      locator: "Lecture 1, §1.2, equations (24)–(35), PDF pp. 4–6; the notes state Q-invariance and off-shell requirements but do not prove the continuum measure identity."
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Example
+
+Use the adopted round-$S^4_r$ $\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ model with one adjoint hypermultiplet of real Euclidean mass $m_E$, dimensionless coupling $g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$, theta angle $\theta$, and natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Thus $[r]=\mathrm{mass}^{-1}$, while $m_E$, Cartan parameters, and $\epsilon_i$ have mass dimension one; $\tau=4\pi i/g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2+\theta/(2\pi)$ and $q=e^{2\pi i\tau}$ are dimensionless. Put
+$$\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=\epsilon=r^{-1},\qquad \widetilde m=m_E+\frac{\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2}{2}.$$
+For the fixed-point algebra below, first treat $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2$ and the complexified Coulomb weights as generic equivariant variables. Sum the fixed-point terms as a rational function before imposing the round-sphere values; on the physical contour the source uses $\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$ and imaginary Nekrasov Coulomb weights.
+
+For $U(2)$ the nonzero roots are $\pm\alpha_{12}$, and the Weyl group is $S_2$. Use Pestun's full Lie-algebra integral convention $a\in\mathfrak u(2)$, $(a,a)=-\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf 2}(a^2)$, measure $[da]$, and gauge-volume factor $\operatorname{vol}(U(2))$. Its Cartan rewrite uses the source's Weyl factor $\Delta(a)=\prod_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Roots}(U(2))}\alpha\cdot a$. The $\mathfrak u(2)$ expression below is the displayed convention, so no separate $1/|S_2|$ is inserted there.
+
+Write the real Cartan integration parameters in the source convention as $a_E=(a_1^E,a_2^E)$. The one-loop determinant is evaluated at the analytically continued argument $ia_E$, while the Nekrasov factor uses complexified Coulomb weights $a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}=i a_\alpha^E$, as in the source's continuation. Thus $\alpha_{12}(a_E)=a_1^E-a_2^E$ in the one-loop product and $\alpha_{12}(a^{\mathrm P})=i(a_1^E-a_2^E)$ in the instanton series. The source's regularized one-loop factor is
+$$Z_{1\text{-loop}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}(i a_E;m_E)=
+\prod_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Roots}(U(2))}\prod_{n=1}^{\infty}
+\left[\frac{((\alpha\cdot a_E)^2+\epsilon^2n^2)^2}
+{((\alpha\cdot a_E+m_E)^2+\epsilon^2n^2)
+((\alpha\cdot a_E-m_E)^2+\epsilon^2n^2)}\right]^{n/2}.$$
+It is regularized by the source's Barnes-$G$ prescription after dropping the $a_E$-independent divergent factor. If the integral is rewritten on a Cartan, the Weyl factor is included once, separately from this root product.
+
+For a partition $Y$, use a finite downward-closed subset of $\mathbb Z_{\geq0}^2$ and write $|Y|$ for its box count. The source labels its boxes from $1$; translate $(i,j)$ to $(i-1,j-1)$ for the ADHM theorem, which does not change arm or leg lengths. If $s=(i,j)$ in the source's convention, let $\nu_i(Y)$ be the height of column $i$ and $\nu'_j(Y)$ the length of row $j$, and define $v_Y(s)=\nu_i(Y)-j$ and $h_Y(s)=\nu'_j(Y)-i$; these relative vertical and horizontal lengths may be negative when $s$ is outside $Y$. Let $\vec Y=(Y_1,Y_2)$, $|\vec Y|=|Y_1|+|Y_2|$, and let $a_\alpha^{\mathrm A}=c_1^T(e_\alpha)$ be the additive dual-framing weights in the ADHM convention. Let $a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}$ be Pestun's Coulomb weights in the Nekrasov factor. The exact convention match used here is $a_\alpha^{\mathrm A}=-a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}$: the ADHM framing representation has character $W=\sum_\alpha e_\alpha^{-1}$, whereas Pestun's Cartan element acts in the fundamental representation. In the source convention define
+$$E_{\alpha\beta}(s)=-h_{Y_\beta}(s)\epsilon_1+(v_{Y_\alpha}(s)+1)\epsilon_2+a_\beta^{\mathrm P}-a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}.$$
+The source's formal N=2* instanton factor is
+$$Z_{\mathrm{inst}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}(a_1^{\mathrm P},a_2^{\mathrm P},\widetilde m;\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2,q)=
+\sum_{\vec Y}q^{|\vec Y|}
+\prod_{\alpha,\beta=1}^{2}\prod_{s\in Y_\alpha}
+\frac{(E_{\alpha\beta}(s)-\widetilde m)
+(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-E_{\alpha\beta}(s)-\widetilde m)}
+{E_{\alpha\beta}(s)(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-E_{\alpha\beta}(s))}.$$
+
+Pestun reports the following formal partition-function expression:
+$$Z_{S^4}^{\mathrm{formal}}=
+\frac{1}{\operatorname{vol}(U(2))}\int_{\mathfrak u(2)}[da]\,
+\exp\!\left[-\frac{8\pi^2r^2}{g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2}(a,a)\right]
+Z_{1\text{-loop}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}(ia;m_E)
+\left|Z_{\mathrm{inst}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}
+(ia,\widetilde m;\epsilon_1=r^{-1},\epsilon_2=r^{-1},q)\right|^2.$$
+The absolute square denotes the north-pole instanton factor times the south-pole anti-instanton factor on the physical real slice; after complex continuation the two factors are kept independent. If the noncompact equivariant pushforward and pole-matching assumption stated below is supplied, the charge-one coefficient of the ADHM formal series is
+$$C_1=
+\frac{(\epsilon_1-\widetilde m)(\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)
+(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)
+(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}-\widetilde m)}
+{\epsilon_1\epsilon_2(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)
+(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P})}
++
+\frac{(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)
+(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}-\widetilde m)
+(\epsilon_1-\widetilde m)(\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)}
+{(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)
+(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P})\epsilon_1\epsilon_2}.$$
+This is a source-reported formal calculation with a conditional mathematical one-instanton coefficient, not a locally proved continuum path-integral identity.
+
+The charge-one partition tuples are exactly $((\Box),\varnothing)$ and $(\varnothing,(\Box))$. Each square below is one box $(0,0)$ in the color named beneath it; an empty color has no box. The two cases exhaust total size one and the algebraic verification does not rely on the diagram.
+
+~~~tikz
+\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm]
+  \draw[fill=blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);
+  \draw (0,0) grid (1,1);
+  \node[below] at (0.5,0) {$Y_1=\Box$};
+  \node[below] at (2,0) {$Y_2=\varnothing$};
+  \draw[fill=orange!20] (4,0) rectangle (5,1);
+  \draw (4,0) grid (5,1);
+  \node[below] at (4.5,0) {$Y_2=\Box$};
+  \node[below] at (6,0) {$Y_1=\varnothing$};
+\end{tikzpicture}
+~~~
+
+## Example references
+
+[Pestun, Eq. (1.4), §§4–5, and the noncommutative-$S^4$ qualification](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0712.2824)
+
+[Peelaers, Lecture 1 §1.2 and Lecture 2](https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/3026/attachments/2163/2457/Peelaers-LectureNotes.pdf)
+
+[[post-qftx-s4-n2-localization-model|Adopted N=2* U(2) S4 model]]
+
