## Example

Use the adopted round-$S^4_r$ $\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ model with one adjoint hypermultiplet of real Euclidean mass $m_E$, dimensionless coupling $g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$, theta angle $\theta$, and natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Thus $[r]=\mathrm{mass}^{-1}$, while $m_E$, Cartan parameters, and $\epsilon_i$ have mass dimension one; $\tau=4\pi i/g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2+\theta/(2\pi)$ and $q=e^{2\pi i\tau}$ are dimensionless. Put
$$\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=\epsilon=r^{-1},\qquad \widetilde m=m_E+\frac{\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2}{2}.$$For the fixed-point algebra below, first treat $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2$ and the complexified Coulomb weights as generic equivariant variables. Sum the fixed-point terms as a rational function before imposing the round-sphere values; on the physical contour the source uses $\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$ and imaginary Nekrasov Coulomb weights. For $U(2)$ the nonzero roots are $\pm\alpha_{12}$, and the Weyl group is $S_2$. Use Pestun's full Lie-algebra integral convention $a\in\mathfrak u(2)$, $(a,a)=-\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbf 2}(a^2)$, measure $[da]$, and gauge-volume factor $\operatorname{vol}(U(2))$. Its Cartan rewrite uses the source's Weyl factor $\Delta(a)=\prod_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Roots}(U(2))}\alpha\cdot a$. The $\mathfrak u(2)$ expression below is the displayed convention, so no separate $1/|S_2|$ is inserted there. Write the real Cartan integration parameters in the source convention as $a_E=(a_1^E,a_2^E)$. The one-loop determinant is evaluated at the analytically continued argument $ia_E$, while the Nekrasov factor uses complexified Coulomb weights $a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}=i a_\alpha^E$, as in the source's continuation. Thus $\alpha_{12}(a_E)=a_1^E-a_2^E$ in the one-loop product and $\alpha_{12}(a^{\mathrm P})=i(a_1^E-a_2^E)$ in the instanton series. The source's regularized one-loop factor is$$Z_{1\text{-loop}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}(i a_E;m_E)=
\prod_{\alpha\in\mathrm{Roots}(U(2))}\prod_{n=1}^{\infty}
\left[\frac{((\alpha\cdot a_E)^2+\epsilon^2n^2)^2}
{((\alpha\cdot a_E+m_E)^2+\epsilon^2n^2)
((\alpha\cdot a_E-m_E)^2+\epsilon^2n^2)}\right]^{n/2}.$$It is regularized by the source's Barnes-$G$ prescription after dropping the $a_E$-independent divergent factor. If the integral is rewritten on a Cartan, the Weyl factor is included once, separately from this root product. For a partition $Y$, use a finite downward-closed subset of $\mathbb Z_{\geq0}^2$ and write $|Y|$ for its box count. The source labels its boxes from $1$; translate $(i,j)$ to $(i-1,j-1)$ for the ADHM theorem, which does not change arm or leg lengths. If $s=(i,j)$ in the source's convention, let $\nu_i(Y)$ be the height of column $i$ and $\nu'_j(Y)$ the length of row $j$, and define $v_Y(s)=\nu_i(Y)-j$ and $h_Y(s)=\nu'_j(Y)-i$; these relative vertical and horizontal lengths may be negative when $s$ is outside $Y$. Let $\vec Y=(Y_1,Y_2)$, $|\vec Y|=|Y_1|+|Y_2|$, and let $a_\alpha^{\mathrm A}=c_1^T(e_\alpha)$ be the additive dual-framing weights in the ADHM convention. Let $a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}$ be Pestun's Coulomb weights in the Nekrasov factor. The exact convention match used here is $a_\alpha^{\mathrm A}=-a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}$: the ADHM framing representation has character $W=\sum_\alpha e_\alpha^{-1}$, whereas Pestun's Cartan element acts in the fundamental representation. In the source convention define$$E_{\alpha\beta}(s)=-h_{Y_\beta}(s)\epsilon_1+(v_{Y_\alpha}(s)+1)\epsilon_2+a_\beta^{\mathrm P}-a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}.$$The source's formal N=2* instanton factor is$$Z_{\mathrm{inst}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}(a_1^{\mathrm P},a_2^{\mathrm P},\widetilde m;\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2,q)=
\sum_{\vec Y}q^{|\vec Y|}
\prod_{\alpha,\beta=1}^{2}\prod_{s\in Y_\alpha}
\frac{(E_{\alpha\beta}(s)-\widetilde m)
(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-E_{\alpha\beta}(s)-\widetilde m)}
{E_{\alpha\beta}(s)(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-E_{\alpha\beta}(s))}.$$Pestun reports the following formal partition-function expression:$$Z_{S^4}^{\mathrm{formal}}=
\frac{1}{\operatorname{vol}(U(2))}\int_{\mathfrak u(2)}[da]\,
\exp\!\left[-\frac{8\pi^2r^2}{g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2}(a,a)\right]
Z_{1\text{-loop}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}(ia;m_E)
\left|Z_{\mathrm{inst}}^{\mathcal N=2^*}
(ia,\widetilde m;\epsilon_1=r^{-1},\epsilon_2=r^{-1},q)\right|^2.$$The absolute square denotes the north-pole instanton factor times the south-pole anti-instanton factor on the physical real slice; after complex continuation the two factors are kept independent. If the noncompact equivariant pushforward and pole-matching assumption stated below is supplied, the charge-one coefficient of the ADHM formal series is$$C_1=
\frac{(\epsilon_1-\widetilde m)(\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)
(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)
(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}-\widetilde m)}
{\epsilon_1\epsilon_2(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)
(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P})}
+
\frac{(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)
(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}-\widetilde m)
(\epsilon_1-\widetilde m)(\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)}
{(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)
(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P})\epsilon_1\epsilon_2}.$$This is a source-reported formal calculation with a conditional mathematical one-instanton coefficient, not a locally proved continuum path-integral identity. The charge-one partition tuples are exactly $((\Box),\varnothing)$ and $(\varnothing,(\Box))$. Each square below is one box $(0,0)$ in the color named beneath it; an empty color has no box. The two cases exhaust total size one and the algebraic verification does not rely on the diagram. ~~~tikz \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm] \draw[fill=blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (1,1); \draw (0,0) grid (1,1); \node[below] at (0.5,0) {$Y_1=\Box$}; \node[below] at (2,0) {$Y_2=\varnothing$}; \draw[fill=orange!20] (4,0) rectangle (5,1); \draw (4,0) grid (5,1); \node[below] at (4.5,0) {$Y_2=\Box$}; \node[below] at (6,0) {$Y_1=\varnothing$}; \end{tikzpicture} ~~~ ## Example references [Pestun, Eq. (1.4), §§4–5, and the noncommutative-$S^4$ qualification](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0712.2824) [Peelaers, Lecture 1 §1.2 and Lecture 2](https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/3026/attachments/2163/2457/Peelaers-LectureNotes.pdf) [[post-qftx-s4-n2-localization-model|Adopted N=2* U(2) S4 model]] [[pthm-qftx-qexact-localization-ward-identity|Planned regulated field-theory Ward identity]] [[thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count|ADHM fixed points and the formal equivariant series]] [[def-axiom-of-choice|Full Axiom of Choice]] ## Facts & Assumptions **Given:** The adopted Euclidean $\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ model, its round $S^4_r$ source contour, its dimensionless instanton parameter, and the formal source formula above. For the mathematical fixed-point calculation assume full AC, $\zeta>0$, and algebraically independent equivariant characters. [F1] [[post-qftx-s4-n2-localization-model]]: the physical model is $U(2)$ with one adjoint hypermultiplet of Euclidean mass $m_E$, source coupling and contour, $\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$, $q=e^{2\pi i\tau}$, and an assumed but unconstructed supersymmetric noncommutative $S^4$ regulator matching Nekrasov's regulator near both poles. [F2] [[thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count]]: for the positive-FI framed $U(N)$ ADHM quotient with the stated $T^2\times T_G$ action and dual-framing characters, the fixed points are $N$-tuples of partitions and their tangent Euler factors define a coefficientwise formal series. Its Statement assumes full AC. Its proof derives the fixed-point classification and tangent character locally; the ADHM/monad comparisons and any noncompact pushforward remain explicit conditional inputs. [F3] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: full AC means every family of nonempty sets indexed by an arbitrary set has a choice function. It is carried here only because the actual ADHM theorem Statement assumes it; the one-box algebra uses finite sets. [A1] The planned Ward-identity input for this $\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ model is conditional on a fixed finite-dimensional regulator whose measure and cycle are $Q$-invariant, whose regularization is anomaly-free, whose integrand and parameter derivative satisfy the stated compact-parameter $L^1$ majorant, and whose $Q$-exact boundary integral vanishes. It would imply finite-parameter constancy only; no such interface is currently supplied for this model. [A2] Assume a separately justified $t\to\infty$ saddle limit for the regulator in [A1], with the convergence and sector hypotheses needed to identify its value with a noncompact equivariant pushforward on the positive-FI $U(2)$ ADHM quotient whose fixed-point sum uses the inverse tangent Euler classes in [F2]. The exact integration, support, and compactification hypotheses must be supplied; neither compact ABBV nor the local fixed-point calculation in [F2] supplies them. [A3] Assume a $T$-equivariant identification of the positive-FI ADHM fixed-point series with Pestun's Nekrasov pole factor, using the mass shift and framing-sign convention stated in the Example. The source's supersymmetry-compatible noncommutative deformation on $S^4$ and its agreement with the Nekrasov $\mathbb R^4$ regulator near each pole remain adopted assumptions, not constructions. No field-space measure or regulator-removal proof is used here. [F4] [[pthm-qftx-qexact-localization-ward-identity]]: the actual current theorem is for the massless $\mathcal N=4$ $SU(2)$ round-sphere model and a fixed finite-dimensional regulator; its conclusion is finite-$t$ constancy under that item's hypotheses. It does not supply [A1] for the $\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ model. ## Verification **Given:** [F1]–[F3] and the explicit conditional assumptions [A1]–[A3]. The equation for $Z_{S^4}^{\mathrm{formal}}$ is source-reported in [F1]; this verification does not claim to derive it from a continuum measure. 1.1 For $k=1$ and $N=2$, the only partition pairs are $((\Box),\varnothing)$ and $(\varnothing,(\Box))$. Substituting their characters into [F2] gives the tangent characters $t_1+t_2+e_2/e_1+t_1t_2e_1/e_2$ and $t_1+t_2+e_1/e_2+t_1t_2e_2/e_1$, respectively. Their ADHM-convention weights have Euler products$$E_1^{\mathrm A}=\epsilon_1\epsilon_2(b_2-b_1)(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2+b_1-b_2),\qquad
E_2^{\mathrm A}=\epsilon_1\epsilon_2(b_1-b_2)(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2+b_2-b_1),$$
where $b_\alpha=a_\alpha^{\mathrm A}=c_1^T(e_\alpha)$. Substituting $b_\alpha=-a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}$ gives $E_1^{\mathrm A}=\epsilon_1\epsilon_2(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P})$ and $E_2^{\mathrm A}=\epsilon_1\epsilon_2(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P})$, exactly the two source denominators in $C_1$. [given, F2, F3, algebra]

2.1 For the first one-box tuple, the contributing weights in the displayed Nekrasov factor are $E_{11}=\epsilon_2$ and $E_{12}=a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2$. Thus its hypermultiplet numerator is $(\epsilon_1-\widetilde m)(\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)(a_2^{\mathrm P}-a_1^{\mathrm P}+\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2-\widetilde m)(a_1^{\mathrm P}-a_2^{\mathrm P}-\widetilde m)$, and its vector denominator is $E_1^{\mathrm A}$ from step 1.1. For the second color the two Coulomb weights interchange and the denominator is $E_2^{\mathrm A}$. Adding these two fixed-point contributions gives exactly $C_1$ in the Example. This is a finite formal coefficient calculation, not an integration over the noncompact quotient. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 The exponent in the Lie-algebra integral is dimensionless because $[r]=\mathrm{mass}^{-1}$, $[a]=\mathrm{mass}$, and $g_{\mathrm{YM}}$ is dimensionless. The one-loop product uses only ratios of mass-squared terms; each N=2* instanton numerator and denominator has the same mass degree, and $q$ is dimensionless. The Haar-volume and matrix-measure normalization remains the source's stated convention. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 The exponent in the Lie-algebra integral is dimensionless because $[r]=\mathrm{mass}^{-1}$, $[a]=\mathrm{mass}$, and $g_{\mathrm{YM}}$ is dimensionless. The one-loop product uses only ratios of mass-squared terms; each N=2* instanton numerator and denominator has the same mass degree, and $q$ is dimensionless. The Haar-volume and matrix-measure normalization remains the source's stated convention. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 Under [A1]–[A3], the formal coefficient is identified with the charge-one contribution at each source pole. First add the generic fixed-point terms, then specialize to $\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$, $\widetilde m=m_E+r^{-1}$, and $a_\alpha^{\mathrm P}=i a_\alpha^E$; this is Pestun's round-sphere N=2* convention. The south-pole anti-instanton uses $\bar q$ and the conjugate continued parameters. On the physical real slice this is the product represented by $|Z_{\mathrm{inst}}|^2$. Without these assumptions, only the formal ADHM coefficient in step 2.1 has been established locally; the full $S^4$ expression itself remains source-reported. [given, A1, A2, A3, F1, step 2.1]

3.2 At $m_E=0$, one has $\widetilde m=(\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2)/2$; for the round-sphere specialization $\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2$, the charge-one numerator vanishes. This agrees with the source's $\mathcal N=4$ limit and does not assert a separate result for a non-round $\Omega$ background. [given, F1, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 The actual theorem [F4] has a massless $\mathcal N=4$ $SU(2)$ finite-regulator scope, so it cannot supply the planned $\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ Ward input [A1]. Even [A1] would give only finite-$t$ constancy; the large-$t$ saddle and noncompact pushforward [A2] and the pole and regulator matching [A3] remain additional assumptions. Thus the matrix expression remains the source-reported formal example stated above, not a theorem asserting existence of a continuum $U(2)$ theory. [given, A1, A2, A3, F4, step 3.1] ∎

